%[text] %[text:anchor:T_AA4F5AD9] # Getting Started with IAM Explorer
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"**Table of Contents**"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_34BF4BA1] ## Using the data explorer App
%[text] The best way to explore databases is to simply use the app
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] IAMDataExplorer
%[text] ```
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_94E4B7A0] ## Create a Connection
%[text] The connection can be created with a default selected scenario or completely empty
c = iam.data.IIASAconnection("ngfs_phase_3");
%[text] At any point in time, a user can view all the available environments in the database:
c.getEnvironments %[output:08291f61]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4CB4BD86] ## Exploring the databases
%[text] Although the Connection class allows you to perform various REST calls, the best way to explore a database is to use the enviornment class:
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F3279359] ### View Models, Scenarios, and Variables
e = iam.IAMEnvironment(c) %[output:8fb1ba03]
%%
%[text] This object loads by default all the available Models, Scenarios, Variables, Regions and Runs in the database. For example, you can view all the models by running:
head(e.Variables) %[output:01a6e8d8]
%%
%[text] The environment also allows you to change the underlying database:
e.changeEnvironment('iamc15');
disp(e) %[output:8a0ab2c2]
%%
head(e.Models) %[output:941a8dec]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D3D64321] ### Querying Datasets
%[text] There are different options to query and select timeseries within the database:
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_08E63D66] #### Both Model and Scenario are known:
%[text] In that case, one can request all the information from the database:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] data = e.getTimeSeries('model',"GCAM 5.2",'scenario','Current policies (Hot house world, Rep)')
%[text] ```
%[text] However, this query can be quite length as there are many different variables and regions that will be requested. Alternatively, the data can be fildtered down by "regions" and "variables as:
ts = e.getTimeSeries('model','AIM/CGE 2.0','scenario','ADVANCE_2020_1.5C-2100','regions','World','Variables','Emissions|CO2');
%%
%[text] The data returned by the server is always stored in an array of IAM Timeseries objects. Each of these objects will allow you to automatically plot the data by running:
ts(1:2).plot('LineWidth',2); %[output:36fa97c4]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D465DFF6] #### Query by variable / region:
%[text] If neither model or scenario are known, it is also possible to query the data using only the filter values, for example:
e.changeEnvironment("ngfs_phase_3");
%%
ts = e.getTimeSeries(variables = "Emissions|CO", regions = "World");
[ts.Variable]' %[output:017e6ee4]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F02303D0] If multiple datrasets are returned, the plot command will plot all, or a subsection of them:
[p,l] = ts(1:2).plot(LineWidth = 2); %[output:07ad0b58]
l.Location = 'northeast'; %[output:07ad0b58]
%[text] For simplicity, the legend will only plot those variables that are different in each curve. For example, the previous plot have equal model, scenario, run, and region. So the legend only reports the different variable.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B7243D3D] ### Strict versus relaxed queries
%[text] By default, the queries will report any partial match to the dataset you enter. For example, the previous query returned all variables containing Emissions|CO. However, if we wanted a strict match to our inputs we could run:
ts = e.getTimeSeries(variables = "Emissions|CO", regions = "World", strict = true);
ts(1:2).plot('LineWidth', 2); %[output:90b4f1f3]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_05A5A465] ### Filtering variables
%[text] The getTimeSeries only accepts a single "strict" command. However, we can still manually filter our results by running:
e.filterVariables('emissions',false) %[output:0f167285]
e.filterVariables('Emissions|CO',true) %[output:4545bb3a]
%[text] This same process can be done with regions
e.filterRegions('japan',false) %[output:609dd7e7]
e.filterRegions('State of Japan',true) %[output:36e15380]
%[text] and with model/scenarios to find out exact runs:
runs = e.filterRuns('models', "GCAM", 'scenarios', "delayed", 'strict', false);
e.RunList(ismember(e.RunList.run_id,runs),1:8) %[output:4e23319c]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E2A14B46] ## Working with the IAM Timeseries
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D0FD3995] ### Plotting Data
%[text] A stack of IAMTimeseries objects can be plot in two main ways: line and bar plots. All plotting functions have two outputs: the array of graphics objects, and the legened. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0F86FE69] #### Line Plots
%[text] The data in the object can be plotted as a set of line plots. The plot funciton accepts the exact same options as the standard plot function in MATLAB. For example:
figure; %[output:3ed8389f]
ts(1:2).plot('LineWidth',2); %[output:3ed8389f]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_86F81CCF] #### Bar Plots
%[text] Alternatively, the data in the object can also be plotted as a set of bar plots. The bar funciton accepts the exact same options as the standard bar function in MATLAB.
figure; %[output:43ced5fa]
[~,l] = ts(1).bar(); %[output:43ced5fa]
l.Location = 'northeast'; %[output:43ced5fa]
%[text] One useful utility consists in making a stack plot of several data. For example:
figure; %[output:6994d340]
[h,l] = bar(ts(1:2),'stacked'); %[output:6994d340]
l.Location = 'northeast'; %[output:6994d340]
%[text] 

